 that i wanted to introduce now as my  wrapping up words yeah so king c4 also  wins in the exact same way you guys are  absolutely right you go king c4 king d4  because these two squares are ambiguous  right they act as the same square  either king c4 or king d4 both win the  game in this case  um these are the three main  basic systems of corresponding squares  that  were introduced in comprehensive chess  endings that where i got most of this  material  from highly highly recommend that book  and these are the three main systems  that make up  the vast vast majority uh almost all  of the corresponding squares studies  that you'll run into and positions  involving corresponding squares  now of course it does get more complex  than one of these three systems there  are positions  where uh the most common more  complicated case  is you'll run into positions where with  this shortest path  you'll get two of these systems that  sort of are halfway interlinked  so if you get a halfway interlinked uh  quadratic system like you have a  quadratic system here  quadratic system here and they're  sharing this one middle file  you get a six square system which is  pretty intuitive  and you can get two eight square systems  actually  uh connected to each other to make a 13  square system  but they're all combinations of those  basic  systems that we looked at so these are  the basic building blocks  and i think this is enough to solve i'm  going to say like 90  of corresponding squares studies that  you're going to come across  if you do want to go that extra mile and  learn the rest of them  i recommend getting that book by uh by  averbach and again the chapter i believe  is by mikhail  zarin but i think this is where i'm  going to conclude  my study of corresponding squares on  this youtube channel we went  pretty in depth here um  like i said those three basic patterns  are what make up i'm gonna say ninety  percent  ninety plus percent of corresponding  squares end games and the other ones  tend to be combinations of those basic  patterns kind of put together  you can of course also make things more  complicated by giving both sides like an  extra tempi  then both sides have the option to  transpose into other systems of  corresponding squares  that's another way to make it more  complicated and then there  are a just a few really oddball studies  that seemingly for no reason at all  changed the system of corresponding  squares ever so slightly with two  squares being shifted over one square  being shifted over  but those are very very few and far  between they're very much the exceptions  to this rule which holds  almost entirely true all the time so any  final questions for me on corresponding  squares  before we wrap this one up and call it  quits on our study any final questions  so jk rowling asks would this concept  work with aid square  versus quadratic if one side has the  eight square system  available and the other side has the  quadratic system available you have to  just choose the quadratic system  uh that goes back to  uh one of these goes back to one of  these  uh i think this one would accurately  show it  um so  black has now this would wouldn't  actually wouldn't accurately show it  not sure if i could show you an example  here but  uh it would just default to the  quadratic system the eight square  system only works if both sides have all  eight squares available to them just  trust me on that one i guess  so yeah that is the book peter peterson  uh comprehensive  chess endings is of course the series  and then volume four  is the one on pawn endings and there is  that chapter on corresponding squares in  there as well  do all of the top chess players use  these concepts so there are a variety of  ways  that uh people have  used a variety of methods that people  have used to  find corresponding squares and positions  verretsky has  this system of you know looking at mind  squares  but that one is very much on a  case-by-case basis and you sort of have  to examine the entire position  to figure it out which is why i really  like this system  laid out by zarin and offerbox book  because it's it's it's more of a system  right you take a look at a pattern you  recognize what's going on  and then you can build your system out  from there  so the problem is uh jk you would have  that extra space you would have extra  space to extend out your quadratic  system  but it wouldn't really be an eight  square system it would just be an  extension of the quadratic system  right because your eight squares would  have nothing to correspond to  there's no there's no corresponding  square on the other side of the board it  would just be an extension  of the quadratic system um  all right i am going to call it quits  here  uh thank you guys so much for sticking  with me on these corresponding squares  lectures i know they are  really heavy stuff and they are sort of  brain melters  but uh i have a lot of fun going over  these hopefully  you learned a lot about corresponding  squares be sure to join us  on twitch now as i do the  final class for the night analyze your  games that is it for youtube  as always my name is caleb denby and i  want to thank you  very much
 

